# The Nano's 5V pin is 4.76 V on USB, one Schottky drop under the host's 5.0 V. On a typical USB
# port a real board reads 4.55 V to 4.8 V, and each band in volts holds over that range. The
# dividers scale with the pin, so where the LED switches does not move.
# At 300 lx the LDR node is 2.52 V and the threshold is 1.54 V, so the LED is off.
open
step 50ms
expect @led1 off
expect @nano.D2 low
expect @nano.D2 < 0.2V
expect @u1.IN1_N > 2.40V
expect @u1.IN1_N < 2.56V
expect @u1.IN1_P > 1.46V
expect @u1.IN1_P < 1.57V
expect @u1.OUT1 < 0.008V
expect serial contains "light 1"
# 80 lx is between the two thresholds, so the output stays low for this whole step.
set light 80
step 200ms
expect @led1 off
expect @nano.D2 < 0.2V
expect @u1.IN1_N > 1.53V
expect @u1.IN1_N < 1.63V
# 10 lx puts the node at 0.61 V, under both thresholds, so the LED comes on.
set light 10
step --until @led1 lit --timeout 100ms
step 50ms
expect @led1 lit
expect @nano.D2 high
expect @nano.D2 > 3.0V
expect @u1.OUT1 > 3.14V
expect @u1.OUT1 < 3.39V
expect @u1.IN1_N > 0.575V
expect @u1.IN1_N < 0.620V
expect @u1.IN1_P > 1.61V
expect @u1.IN1_P < 1.72V
expect serial contains "dark 2"
# The same 80 lx, reached from the dark, keeps the LED on.
set light 80
step 200ms
expect @led1 lit
expect @nano.D2 > 3.0V
expect @u1.IN1_P > 1.61V
expect @u1.IN1_P < 1.72V
set light 300
step --until @led1 off --timeout 100ms
step 50ms
expect @led1 off
expect @nano.D2 low
expect @nano.D2 < 0.2V
expect serial contains "light 3"
expect no diagnostics
